1
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:03,740
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:05,520
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:05,940
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,080
And my name is Katherine.

5
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:09,400
And today we're talking about something very serious.

6
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:10,600
Very serious.

7
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,780
Today we're talking about capital punishment.

8
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,680
So if you've never heard that phrase before, you might have heard another name for this topic.

9
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:19,520
It's called the death penalty.

10
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:20,100
Right.

11
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:26,500
So if you commit a crime and basically the judge says that you will have to be put to death for that crime.

12
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:26,940
All right.

13
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:28,500
So many countries no longer have this.

14
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:33,200
This law, the United States, you can see it in some places, some states, but not in others.

15
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:38,900
So what we're talking about today is the debate between to have the death penalty and to not have it.

16
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:39,440
All right.

17
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:45,740
So we're going to actually have a little debate in the end of if we agree that we should have the death penalty or not.

18
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:49,800
But before that, why don't we listen to this dialogue for the first time?

19
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:58,080
That's all for today's class.

20
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:01,680
We will continue our lecture on crime and punishment tomorrow.

21
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,600
Do you think we should be tougher on crime?

22
00:01:09,790 --> 00:01:11,410
Well, it depends on what you mean.

23
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:19,950
For example, we could bring back the death penalty for murder, give longer prison sentences for lesser offenses and lock up juvenile offenders.

24
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:22,950
Those sound like really draconian measures.

25
00:01:23,610 --> 00:01:28,310
Firstly, what do you do about miscarriages of justice if you've already put innocent people to death?

26
00:01:28,930 --> 00:01:33,650
You'd only use capital punishment if you were absolutely sure that you convicted the right person.

27
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:38,290
But there have been many cases of wrongful conviction where people have been imprisoned for many years.

28
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:42,710
The authorities were sure at the time, but later it was shown that the evidence was unreliable.

29
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:46,230
In some cases, it had been fabricated by the police.

30
00:01:46,830 --> 00:01:54,010
Well, no system of justice can be perfect, but surely there's a good case for longer prison sentences to deter serious crime.

31
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:58,670
I doubt whether they could act as an effective deterrent while the detection rate is so low.

32
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:03,520
The best way to prevent crime is to convince people who commit it that they're going to be caught.

33
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,850
It doesn't make sense to divert all your resources into the prison system.

34
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:09,650
But if you detect more crimes, you'll still need prisons.

35
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:15,230
In my reckoning, if we could lock up more juvenile criminals, they'd learn that they can't get away with it.

36
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,240
Soft sentences will merely encourage them to do it again.

37
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:23,100
Yes, but remember that prisons are often schools for criminals.

38
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,120
To remove crime from society, you really have to tackle its causes.

39
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:31,060
Well, if I were president, I would impose tougher laws and punishment.

40
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:36,900
I would have a peaceful society based on fear of punishment, not consciousness of doing the right thing.

41
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:38,680
You sound like a dictator.

42
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:40,660
Well, if it works, why not?

43
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:49,800
All right, so Marco, there's a lot of really great legal terms here, legal and crime terms.

44
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:52,600
And so let's look at a few of these in today's Language Takeaway.

45
00:02:54,510 --> 00:02:55,850
Language Takeaway.

46
00:02:57,040 --> 00:02:59,140
All right, so let's take a look at the first one.

47
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,200
They mentioned juvenile offenders.

48
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:07,420
All right, so let's look at these two words because, well, offender can be pretty general.

49
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,320
It's someone who commits a crime.

50
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:11,680
So an offender.

51
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:13,240
But we're talking about a special kind of offender.

52
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:15,060
Here we're talking about juvenile offenders.

53
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,080
So they're young offenders.

54
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:18,080
Right.

55
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:21,640
In America, legally, you're a juvenile offender if you're under the age of 18.

56
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:27,100
Okay, so if you commit a crime while you are a juvenile, you get judged differently, right?

57
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:29,040
You don't get the same treatment as an adult.

58
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,040
Right.

59
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:38,020
Usually, the punishments are not as harsh because people like to think that younger people, younger children or teenagers, they don't really understand the law.

60
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,640
They don't really understand crime or good and bad.

61
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,320
So it should be easier on them.

62
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:44,740
All right, very good.

63
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:50,040
So we have juvenile offenders and we also have wrongful conviction.

64
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:57,240
All right, this is an uh-oh moment for a judge because a wrongful conviction, well, conviction first of all is a decision.

65
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:01,500
So this is a court's decision on whether or not a person is guilty.

66
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:03,280
So you convicted the person.

67
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:03,700
Right.

68
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:08,400
Marco was convicted guilty of stealing my computer.

69
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:09,720
I was too.

70
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:11,280
That happened.

71
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:11,840
Right.

72
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:19,160
But it's a wrongful conviction if Marco didn't really steal a computer but we decided that he did.

73
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:20,160
Okay.

74
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,920
So it was a wrong decision.

75
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:22,580
Right.

76
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:26,480
It was a bad decision in the sense that it's actually not what happened in reality.

77
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:28,820
A wrongful conviction.

78
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:30,200
Very good.

79
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:36,920
And actually related to a wrongful conviction, many times it happens because evidence is fabricated by the police.

80
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:38,040
Right.

81
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:39,040
So this is a big no-no.

82
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,800
This is something that really shouldn't happen and it's against the law in many countries.

83
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:49,600
So fabrication or to fabricate is to fake, to make something up.

84
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:58,200
And so evidence that's fabricated is evidence that the police or other people create to try and make someone look guilty but it's not actually evidence.

85
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:59,200
It's not really.

86
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,200
That's not true.

87
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:00,470
It's not true.

88
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:02,200
Right.

89
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:06,110
And now moving on, they were talking about a deterrent for crime.

90
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,680
Okay, so to deter is a verb.

91
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:12,240
To deter means to try and get someone to not do something.

92
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:12,840
All right.

93
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:18,520
So I'm going to try and deter my staff from taking early lunch breaks.

94
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:21,100
So I'm going to punish someone as a deterrent.

95
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:22,320
As a deterrent.

96
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:23,960
Okay.

97
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:27,480
So you have the verb deter and you have the noun a deterrent.

98
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:28,480
Right.

99
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:29,480
Yeah.

100
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,600
And so oftentimes very strict laws are meant to be deterrents.

101
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:37,010
They're meant to keep people from doing bad things, from breaking the law.

102
00:05:37,390 --> 00:05:37,630
All right.

103
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:41,150
So for example, capital punishment is one of those deterrents.

104
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:44,650
If you kill somebody, then if you get caught, you are also going to be killed.

105
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:49,240
So that means I'm never going to kill anyone because I don't want the law to decide that I'm going to get killed.

106
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:50,240
Exactly.

107
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:53,710
And our last word for today, they were talking about imposing tougher laws.

108
00:05:53,970 --> 00:05:55,450
I would impose tougher laws.

109
00:05:55,770 --> 00:05:56,090
Okay.

110
00:05:56,150 --> 00:06:01,450
So to impose is a verb that means to put in place or to set up or enact.

111
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:02,960
And so...

112
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,360
You don't really impose, right?

113
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:06,240
You don't really agree to do it.

114
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:08,750
Like the dictator imposes his will.

115
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:09,450
All right.

116
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,270
So he makes everyone do what he wants.

117
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,650
But we can impose measures that do something.

118
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:21,490
It's kind of like forcibly making people follow some kind of rule or follow a person.

119
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:27,950
Now sometimes you would see that you have a company or you have people at your house and they're like, well, you know, stay over for dinner.

120
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:30,510
And they're like, oh, I wouldn't want to impose.

121
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:31,330
All right.

122
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:40,280
Well, I wouldn't want to bother you or I wouldn't want to create more work for you because, you know, when you're making food, you have all these people, they don't want to impose on you.

123
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:41,280
All right.

124
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:42,890
So that's another way of using this, impose on somebody.

125
00:06:43,370 --> 00:06:43,830
All right.

126
00:06:43,850 --> 00:06:49,310
So after all of these words that we've taken a look at, why don't we start now with Fluency Builder?

127
00:06:50,670 --> 00:06:51,390
Fluency Builder.

128
00:06:52,050 --> 00:06:52,370
Okay.

129
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:58,770
So as you know, Fluency Builder is a place where we get to talk about some phrases and words that allow you to be more fluent in English.

130
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:03,760
So these are sometimes idioms or sometimes phrases, words that go together commonly.

131
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:05,080
And the first one is no exception.

132
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:05,940
It's a miscarriage.

133
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:08,440
So miscarriage of justice.

134
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:09,440
All right.

135
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:11,040
A miscarriage of justice.

136
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:13,000
Now we have different words here.

137
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:16,280
The first one, miscarriage, it's usually related to a medical term.

138
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:17,280
Right.

139
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:18,280
It's a pregnancy.

140
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:20,160
Well, it's a pregnancy that has a sad result.

141
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:24,080
It's a pregnancy where the woman's baby dies or the woman loses the baby.

142
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:27,420
And a miscarriage of justice is a metaphor.

143
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:31,460
It's a way to say that justice was not carried out.

144
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:38,560
It means that, for example, with those wrongful convictions, the law failed.

145
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,280
To accomplish its objective.

146
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:40,720
Yeah.

147
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:42,660
To accomplish its objective and to be fair.

148
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:43,520
Okay.

149
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:45,220
So a miscarriage of justice.

150
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:46,220
Interesting.

151
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:55,840
And moving on to our next phrase, they were talking and discussing this whole death penalty situation and she mentioned, in my reckoning.

152
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:56,820
All right.

153
00:07:56,840 --> 00:08:01,180
So this is a great way to share your opinion or your idea with someone.

154
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:09,600
You're saying pretty much, in my opinion, in my, according to me or, you know, the way I'm thinking is this.

155
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:12,240
So I can also say, I reckon, right?

156
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:13,560
Exactly.

157
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:15,260
I reckon it's going to rain later.

158
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:16,580
I think it's going to rain.

159
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:18,760
So in my reckoning, in my opinion.

160
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:19,600
All right.

161
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:20,680
I reckon.

162
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:28,270
And to finish off Fluency Builder, they said that the person or the criminal couldn't get away with it.

163
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:29,070
All right.

164
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:32,530
So this is another one of those phrases that each word maybe means something different.

165
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:33,990
You can get somewhere.

166
00:08:35,300 --> 00:08:45,120
But put together, to get away with something means to do something bad or illegal or that's not okay without getting caught.

167
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:45,710
Or being punished.

168
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:46,510
Or being punished.

169
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:49,360
So you can say, get away with murder.

170
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:50,360
He got away with murder.

171
00:08:51,220 --> 00:08:55,200
You know, he did something that was really, really bad but no one caught him.

172
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:59,120
And also it means the criminal got away so they couldn't catch him.

173
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,220
Or I could always say to my brother, he would always lie to my parents and get away with it.

174
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:05,860
So he never got caught.

175
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:06,840
He never got punished.

176
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:07,460
Okay.

177
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:10,260
So he got away with his lie or got away, got his way.

178
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:11,280
All right.

179
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:13,120
So I think this is an interesting phrase.

180
00:09:13,400 --> 00:09:15,340
Why don't we listen to a couple of more examples.

181
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:20,360
Example one.

182
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:24,720
Do you think we could get away with using the cheaper product?

183
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:27,640
Example two.

184
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:30,700
Thieves got away with two Picassos which were never found.

185
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,720
Example three.

186
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:37,560
He almost got away with murder.

187
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:39,750
Unfortunately for him, the police discovered his secret.

188
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,240
So we've taken a look at a lot of great phrases.

189
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:47,640
Why don't we listen to our dialogue for the last time and then we'll come back.

190
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:57,430
That's all for today's class.

191
00:09:58,170 --> 00:10:01,050
We will continue our lecture on crime and punishment tomorrow.

192
00:10:01,530 --> 00:10:08,360
Do you think we should be tougher on crime?

193
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:10,680
Well it depends on what you mean.

194
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:19,300
For example, we could bring back the death penalty for murder, give longer prison sentences for lesser offenses, and lock up juvenile offenders.

195
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:22,300
Those sound like really draconian measures.

196
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:27,680
Firstly, what do you do about miscarriages of justice if you've already put innocent people to death?

197
00:10:28,260 --> 00:10:33,040
You'd only use capital punishment if you were absolutely sure that you convicted the right person.

198
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:37,640
But there have been many cases of wrongful conviction where people have been imprisoned for many years.

199
00:10:38,280 --> 00:10:42,060
The authorities were sure at the time, but later it was shown that the evidence was unreliable.

200
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:45,580
In some cases, it had been fabricated by the police.

201
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:53,360
Well, no system of justice can be perfect, but surely there's a good case for longer prison sentences to deter serious crime.

202
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:58,040
I doubt whether they could act as an effective deterrent while the detection rate is so low.

203
00:10:58,560 --> 00:11:02,880
The best way to prevent crime is to convince people who commit it that they're going to be caught.

204
00:11:02,880 --> 00:11:06,200
It doesn't make sense to divert all your resources into the prison system.

205
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:09,000
But if you detect more crimes, you'll still need prisons.

206
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:14,580
In my reckoning, if we could lock up more juvenile criminals, they'd learn that they can't get away with it.

207
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:18,600
Soft sentences will merely encourage them to do it again.

208
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:22,480
Yes, but remember that prisons are often schools for criminals.

209
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:26,440
To remove crime from society, you really have to tackle its causes.

210
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:30,400
Well, if I were president, I would impose tougher laws and punishment.

211
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:36,240
I would have a peaceful society based on fear of punishment, not consciousness of doing the right thing.

212
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:38,020
You sound like a dictator.

213
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:40,020
Well, if it works, why not?

214
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:51,200
All right, so now the big question, Catherine, do you agree with the death penalty?

215
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:58,980
Oh, this is a big one, and I don't think we have enough time to go into it in much detail here, but I definitely think that is extreme, right?

216
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:01,640
And so we have this phrase, draconian measures.

217
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:03,600
Right, they mentioned that in the dialogue.

218
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:08,020
Yeah, so draconian, something that's draconian is excessive or severe, you know?

219
00:12:08,840 --> 00:12:23,960
And I think that there are a lot of reasons not to have the death penalty, because the death penalty, well, you're pretty much saying we as a society think that murder is wrong, but we're going to show you that you've done a wrong thing, like a bad thing that's against the law by killing you.

220
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,300
All right, and so I have to disagree with that on a philosophical level.

221
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:29,940
But what about practical?

222
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:37,860
Practically speaking, I think that there are a lot of problems with the prison system and the legal system in the United States, for example, because prisons are really crowded.

223
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:40,160
1% of the American population is in prison.

224
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:45,820
And so I think that so long as the prison system still has problems, there's a really good argument for having the death penalty.

225
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:52,400
But still, I think that it's excessive and it's unnecessary and that there are other ways to treat and punish prisoners.

226
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:59,450
It is an interesting topic because you have many different angles that are in favor or against the death penalty.

227
00:12:59,450 --> 00:13:04,010
For example, you have religious views where you say that you should not take a life of another person no matter what.

228
00:13:04,350 --> 00:13:10,810
Or you have other views that say that the death penalty is simply wrong and that you can't right a wrong with another wrong.

229
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:12,320
Right, exactly.

230
00:13:12,320 --> 00:13:13,530
So it kind of doesn't make sense.

231
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:23,600
But at the same time, you do, like you say, the prisons are full of people and it costs around I think it's $12,000 a year per person to keep them in prison.

232
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:29,360
So what do you do with all of these people that have life sentences that are going to spend the rest of their lives in jail?

233
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:37,280
So it is interesting and many countries are very, very strict with their legal system in comparison with the United States, right?

234
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:45,260
Definitely, but there is a statistic that's pretty stunning that I think among the top 20 developed nations in the world, I think only the United States is the one with the death penalty.

235
00:13:45,400 --> 00:13:45,820
Exactly.

